ot of experience doing a  multiplex app on  starter chemistry preps and  i haven't actually tried ultimate  technology on it so i would be curious  on what  um cathedral would say about it but in  my experience with multiplexifone  photochemistry  it's really highly depend on how the  samples are our cells rather are fixed  and  prepped as i think we had an earlier  question about  looking at the different cellular spaces  membrane cytoplasm nucleus  typically with cytochemistry or cell  preps  those membranes can be easily disrupted  and  can diminish membrane signal to get into  the nuclei and stand for nuclear signal  so there's a tricky balance and there's  kind of multiple fixation strategies  that  can be applied to get the best of both  worlds  but i can't speak specifically on the uh  ultimate plex for cytochemistry itself  yeah so from my perspective we haven't  had it we actually haven't tested that  yet  it's something that we've discussed  internally and it may be something that  we evaluate  at a later date but right now we don't  have any data to say how  successful we were not at stating those  types of samples  thank you now we have time for a few  more questions but i do want to remind  our audience that those questions that  we are unable to answer  live today and those that do come in  during the on demand period  they will be answered by our speakers  via the email address you provided  at the time of registration we do have  quite a few  questions that have come in and we for  sure will not be able to get to them  today  but they will be answered so let's  continue  can the ultimate assay be used on fresh  sections that have not been embedded in  paraffin  uh we don't test that internally  formally um but we have some customers  that are evaluating that right now as we  speak  um and it kind of speaks to the same  point scott had just made um you know  that they will require some fixation  processing um  in that you know working that out and  optimizing that is what would be  required to see if our assay would work  optimally there or not  um another consideration to take there  too is that most standard ihc  antibodies are derived against epitopes  that have  undergone specific types of fixation so  sometimes secondary sets of antibodies  need to be used in order to  optimally stain epitopes within fresh  frozen meaning  an antibody that works really well and  ffpe may not work  as well in fresh frozen  is there a trial and error required to  eliminate background  uh from our uh optimization perspective  not really um you know we really  optimize a primary antibody first and  then we really just need to  optimize the appropriate amount of  imaging strands  but that's pretty turn key at this point  for the amount of development we've done  on the numerous markers that we have  yeah and just the background here that  um so  you know our experience with uh the  ultimate institute versus conventional  is  a lot of that's been taken care of which  is really a  a large weight off of the lab trying to  as i mentioned optimize on it's very  difficult to get that and exclude  background and a noise  the turnkey solution the ultimate has  been tremendously helpful and  i think that's a very um a  strong advantage of the technology and  then in the cleaner application  which reduces a lot of that issue  yeah maybe maybe scott you could comment  on how much time it typically took your  lab  uh to develop a standard you know four  plex assay or something using some other  type of maybe chromogenic multiplex  or other force and platform sure yeah i  mean we so we do it chromogenic um  generally as i kind of mentioned in my  presentation we don't go  above two too much because then all  those uh the chromosomes can sort of  blend  and it gets more difficult to  deconvolute the  signal from one another but with  fluorescence um  of course the issue with fluorescence is  not just  optimized native by concentration you  also have that possibly the secondary  concentration  once you directly conjugate it but then  you also have very low signal  so it's really a balance of signal to  the affinity ability of the target  that you're looking at but then you also  have to balance it against which  channel that works best in relative to  your antibody  and then you have to look at according  to your on-prem equipment  to make sure it balances out and spec  that out properly  that process just to get set up is  pretty uh time-consuming  but i think in our experience we were  routinely six or eight months to get a  fourplex panel  iaf into production um and a significant  amount of effort on our team  thank you gentlemen and we'll wrap up  this final question what kind of  expertise is needed  to use your technology  so i think that's one of the beauties of  our technology um is that we know and it  kind of spins off of what scott was just  referring to is that we really uh  pre-optimize these assays for you  meaning the development and optimization  of each individual marker and then the  multiplex afterwards  is something that you do not need to do  and that in the past has required a lot  of expertise and a lot of time as scott  had mentioned  in order to do so so you know being able  to deliver pre-optimized kits is  something we can do fast and quick  and assuming the site has um you know  the proper infrastructure to do the  scanning and the scanning  um you know we can really get anybody up  and trained on how to run this essay  thank you both for this excellent q a  session do either of you have any final  comments for our audience  sure i just want to say thank you to  everybody for taking the time to  sit on this webinar today and i want to  say a big thank you to scott and the  lido's team  um you know as i mentioned before you  know they were really the impetus for us  to  develop this new aplex workflow for the  field you know as i said before  something that we had just kept as 